{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 36, :comments ["<b>And its owner did not guard it.</b> It is known that even regarding the tam, if its owner guarded it properly and it went out and caused damage by happenstance that occurred, he is certainly exempt. So when Scripture says regarding the mu'ad <b>and did not guard it</b>, according to the opinion stated in the Talmud that the mu'ad requires superior guarding compared to the tam (Bava Kamma 45a), Scripture says: <b>And if it is a goring ox and it has been warned (mu'ad) by its owner and he did not guard it</b> — to require additional guarding on account of its viciousness, and if it went out and caused damage, he pays full damages. And according to the opinion that the guarding of the tam and the mu'ad is equal (Bava Kamma 45a, and Tosefta there), Scripture says: <b>And if it becomes known to him that it is a goring ox</b>, and now as well <b>he did not guard it</b> — on account of his great negligence he is liable for full damages." "<b>And the dead animal shall be his.</b> To the injured party, and upon it the one who caused the damage pays until the injured party is fully compensated for his complete damages. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 21:36), following the way of our Sages (Bava Kamma 10a). If so, it is proper that we interpret <b>he shall surely pay ox for ox</b> together with <b>and the dead animal shall be his</b> — similar to \"and Joseph was in Egypt\" (Exodus 1:5), together with Joseph who was already in Egypt; and similarly \"I cannot endure iniquity and solemn assembly\" (Isaiah 1:13), meaning \"solemn assembly,\" as he says \"hating robbery in burnt offering\" (Isaiah 61:8). And it is possible to interpret according to the plain sense: <b>and the dead animal shall be his</b> — to the one who caused the damage, meaning <b>he shall surely pay ox for ox and the dead animal shall be his</b>, to the one who caused the damage who pays with these payments, that he brings it to the injured party into the reckoning. And according to both interpretations the law is the same: the owners deal with the carcass, and from the moment of death it enters into the reckoning with him, according to the words of our Sages (Bava Kamma there)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:13:55.134736479Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}